  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nMid level troughing working slowly east across the northern Great \nLakes early this afternoon...anchored by closed mid level \ncirculation and attendant surface low sliding across southern \nIllinois. Juxtaposition of best forcing and deep layer moisture \nconvergence tied to that anchor point, bringing much more organized \nand substantial rains to the lower Lakes and Ohio Valley. Forcing and \nmoisture convergence not nearly on the same scale in our neck of the \nwoods, keeping our showers much less organized and intense. Plenty \nof clouds however, helping keep our temperatures a bit on the cool \nside of specific normal values....with current reading in the 60s to \nlow/mid 70s.\n\nNearly complete detachment of southern portion of mid level \ntroughing expected as we head into early next week (broad mid level \ncirculation looks to center over the Tennessee Valley) as our \noverhead flow regime remaining quite progressive through the period. \nThis will keep any threat for our typical early August dog days of \nsummer heat locked up well to our west and south. While the vast \nmajority of the extended looks dry, passing waves within that \nprogressive flow pattern will bring periodic chances for additional \nshowers at times.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nShower evolution through Sunday morning...followed by temperature \ntrends and addressing any additional shower threat through the \nremainder of the period.\n\nDetails: \n\nStill expecting some passing showers this afternoon into Sunday \nmorning (perhaps a rumble of thunder or two into early this evening \nalong differential heating boundaries) with passing mid level \ntroughing and weak inverted surface trough. Footprint of showers \nexpected to decrease from northwest to southeast later tonight into \nSunday morning, with the threat for showers largely over with by the \nafternoon. Large area of Canada originated high pressure builds into \nthe Northwoods Sunday night, slowly working across the region Monday \nand Tuesday. This sets up what should be some ideal summer weather, \nwith sun-filled skies, high temperatures in the 70s to middle 80s \n(warmest Tuesday), and low humidity.\n\nFalling mid-level heights and attendant surface trough/cold front \nset to arrive later Tuesday night into Wednesday, likely drumming up \nsome chances for showers and storms in the process. Disconnect \nbetween this forcing and deepest tropical moisture plume to our \nsouth and east should prevent this from becoming a more widespread \nand significant rain event.\n\nTrends support broad long-wave troughing dominating our weather the \nsecond half of the week. Again, this looks to keep any heat well \nremoved from our region. Periodic waves within relative fast flow \naloft will bring at least the threat for additional shower chances \nat times, but not seeing any real evidence of any appreciable \nwidespread heavy rain events as low level streamline wind analysis \nsuggests deepest tropical moisture axis remaining off to our east \nwhere ridge/trough interface will reside.",
